  "verbalization": "Tiotropium, a bronchodilator, is implicated in the treatment of asthma through its interaction with the nervous system, as indicated by the shared lowest common ancestors with Formoterol and Arformoterol, both of which are also bronchodilators. The evidence suggests that Tiotropium may exert its therapeutic effects on chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) through a mechanism involving the nervous system, potentially by modulating pathways that are common to multiple bronchodilators. The recurring connection between Tiotropium and COPD via the nervous system, combined with the shared pathways of Formoterol and Arformoterol, implies a possible role for Tiotropium in COPD treatment through a mechanism that involves the nervous system. The evidence also highlights the importance of the nervous system in the pharmacological action of bronchodilators, suggesting that Tiotropium may be repurposed for COPD treatment through a mechanism that involves the nervous system. This integrated explanation is supported by the overlapping pathways and the shared lowest common ancestors, which collectively suggest a potential link between Tiotropium and COPD through a nervous system-mediated mechanism.",
